.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.TH PUTS 3S
.SH NAME
puts, fputs \- put a string on a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int puts(const char *s);
.PP
.B int fputs(const char *s, FILE *stream);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Puts
copies the null-terminated string
.I s
to the standard output stream
.I stdout
and appends a
newline character.
.PP
.I Fputs
copies the null-terminated string
.I s
to the named output
.IR stream .
.PP
Neither routine copies the terminal null character.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page said nothing about return values, because it had none to report \- v7's
.I fputs
returned whatever fell out.  Both return a non-negative value on success and EOF
on error, as C11 sec 7.21.7.4 and 7.21.7.9 require.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
fopen(3), gets(3),
putc(3), printf(3),
ferror(3), stdio(3)
.br
fread(3) for
.I fwrite
.SH BUGS
.I Puts
appends a newline,
.I fputs
does not, all in the name of backward compatibility.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/puts.c, fputs.c
